WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.244 --> 00:00:02.800
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.970
- [Narrator] Few industries
have been as impacted

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.522
by the coronavirus as
the grocery business.

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:11.460
When lockdowns began, grocery
stores transformed themselves

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.866
physically to respond to safety concerns.

00:00:13.866 --> 00:00:17.221
At the same time, demand skyrocketed,

00:00:17.221 --> 00:00:20.510
pushing food retails sales to record highs

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:24.080
and in many cases, remaking
entire supply chains.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.140
- I've been in this business
since I was a child.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.489
COVID-19 was something
we've never seen before.

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:31.040
- Everybody has been affected,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.485
and I think the industry will be remade

00:00:34.485 --> 00:00:36.690
in the future as a result.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.420
- [Narrator] The pandemic
has triggered a boom

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.980
in online shopping.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.220
Instacart, the nation's
biggest independent

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.870
grocery delivery service
reported that its order volumes

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.194
are up as much as 500% year over year.

00:00:49.194 --> 00:00:51.380
- So what happens next?

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.100
What will be the lasting
legacy of COVID-19

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.430
for the food retail
industry and its workers?

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.833
Simply put, how will we
shop in the near future?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.292
- [Narrator] Plastic shields, masks,

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:07.550
social distancing markers.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.890
If you've visited a grocery store any time

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.738
in the past few months, you've
almost certainly encountered

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:15.770
these new cosmetic changes.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.230
But what about consumer behavior?

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.793
Are we shopping differently?

00:01:21.819 --> 00:01:23.340
- There I am.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.050
- [Narrator] To find out,
I spoke with Bill Bishop,

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.380
a veteran industry analyst.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.550
- Consumers are, first of all,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.686
going to the store less frequently.

00:01:32.686 --> 00:01:36.405
They are rushing through
the shopping experience

00:01:36.405 --> 00:01:41.179
more quickly than normal,
browsing is not fashionable.

00:01:41.179 --> 00:01:42.960
- During the pandemic

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.037
people definitely started
buying different things.

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:48.260
- Kelvin Rodriguez
operates an independent,

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.290
family run grocery store,
Billy's Marketplace,

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.890
in Ridgewood Queens.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.010
- Number one was paper, paper products.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.441
A lot of non perishables and
they were buying more bulk.

00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:00.933
- [Narrator] Before the pandemic,

00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:03.969
local independent stores
like Billy's Marketplace

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:08.484
made up only about 11%
of all grocery sales.

00:02:08.484 --> 00:02:11.950
Most Americans shop at the
nation's biggest chains

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.311
like Walmart, Amazon
Whole Foods, or Kroger.

00:02:15.311 --> 00:02:18.860
- The retail grocery
business is a huge business

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.762
made up of many competitors
which makes it hyper competitive

00:02:22.762 --> 00:02:25.780
and a very low margin business.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.860
- Before the pandemic, grocery
stores in the inner city

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.369
I would say were struggling a bit.

00:02:30.369 --> 00:02:32.810
Right, there was a lot of competition.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.066
There was a lot of big box
stores coming into New York.

00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:37.970
- [Narrator] To cater to the tastes

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.160
of his mostly millennial customers,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.890
Rodriguez set up a beer bar
in a coffee shop in the store.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.690
- [Rodriguez] We wanted
to create an experience

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.836
and that's where we thought
this industry was headed.

00:02:48.836 --> 00:02:51.470
We wanted to create where you could

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.350
almost get everything in one.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.530
We know everyone that's
walking in through that door

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.230
and we have a relationship
with our customers.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.880
- [Narrator] But with New York
still on partial lockdown,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.960
those offerings are simply not possible

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.110
because of safety regulations.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.078
Fortunately, late last year
Rodriguez had the foresight

00:03:08.078 --> 00:03:12.300
to jump on the biggest trend
in food retail, e-commerce.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.800
- Usually we don't know
the difference between

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.310
an Instacart shopper
and a regular shopper.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:19.770
- [Narrator] With such low margins,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.880
it's impractical for smaller operations

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.800
to create their own delivery services.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.240
Instead, if independent
stores like Billy's

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.650
want to sell online,

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.970
they have to look to third
party companies like Shopify,

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.080
Fresh Direct, or Instacart.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.290
- Previous to pandemic,
it was about one percent,

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.740
which you would say one
percent is pretty small.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.570
After the pandemic, it doubled,
it went to two percent.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.600
- [Narrator] Although
a one percent increase

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.400
may not sound like much,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.178
in an industry with such low margins,

00:03:48.178 --> 00:03:51.350
one percent can be the difference
between being profitable

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.138
or simply breaking even.

00:03:53.138 --> 00:03:55.820
In a statement to the Wall Street Journal,

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.917
Instacart wrote, "We believe
that brick and mortar retailers

00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:01.287
"are the backbone of
the future of grocery.

00:04:01.287 --> 00:04:04.347
"people want to shop from the
grocers they love and trust

00:04:04.347 --> 00:04:06.542
"and have gone to for generations."

00:04:06.542 --> 00:04:09.930
Before the pandemic,
online grocery shopping

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.150
was a small fraction of
all grocery shopping.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.550
But since then, it has gone mainstream.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:19.890
- Well, the largest player
in grocery would be Walmart.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:23.300
- [Narrator] Walmart has
approximately 5,000 US stores,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.320
which can be found 10 miles
from 90% of the US population.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.480
- We've been focused in
this space for a while now

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.545
building up our curbside
pickup capabilities

00:04:33.545 --> 00:04:38.315
that are in over 3,300 of our
locations as we stand today

00:04:38.315 --> 00:04:40.550
and growing each week.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:41.780
- [Narrator] According to Ward,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.870
although the trend toward online shopping

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.730
has existed for a while now,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.380
the coronavirus accelerated its adoption.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.893
- [Ward] We heard that
customers felt more comfortable

00:04:50.893 --> 00:04:53.631
if they didn't have to
have a personal exchange.

00:04:53.631 --> 00:04:57.420
All of our curbside interactions
now are contactless.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.027
In fact, our customers don't
even wind their window down

00:05:00.027 --> 00:05:04.240
and the transaction is
conducted inside the vehicle.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.100
Our associates load the trunk
on the customer's behalf.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.423
- [Narrator] More than
38.6 million Americans

00:05:10.423 --> 00:05:13.200
have filed for unemployment since March,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.983
creating a new talent pool
for those expanded operations.

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:20.334
One area of growth has been
in non traditional gig work.

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:23.730
Instacart, for example,
relies on gig workers

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.365
to shop for its customers.

00:05:25.365 --> 00:05:28.470
But like many industries
that rely on gig workers,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.331
the sustainability of this delivery model

00:05:30.331 --> 00:05:33.560
depends heavily on the
worker's compensation

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.952
and whether they are
classified as employees.

00:05:35.952 --> 00:05:40.140
The long term benefits for
retailers with an online presence

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.830
lies in the data they can
collect about their customers.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:48.146
- If a retailer has your
business online and offline

00:05:48.146 --> 00:05:50.880
it gives you a complete
picture of the household

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.751
or a much more complete
picture, which is very valuable.

00:05:54.751 --> 00:05:57.700
- [Narrator] The growth of
e-commerce has also given rise

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:01.630
to dark stores, the name
of which can be misleading.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.460
Dark stores aren't really
stores in a traditional sense

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.860
because they're not open to customers.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.450
They're more like depots that
serve primarily as a location

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:13.130
for pickup or dispersed delivery.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.340
- It's particularly important

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.792
because it's a route profitability.

00:06:16.792 --> 00:06:19.269
The cost of picking the order,

00:06:19.269 --> 00:06:21.170
it's what we call pick and pack,

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.070
is substantially less in a dark store.

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:26.620
- [Narrator] Looking
further into the future,

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.630
a sign of things to come could be

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.320
online only companies like Ocado.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.810
Based in the UK, Ocado
only does home deliveries

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.520
from its warehouses.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.780
- Ocado is a stand alone online service

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:45.135
that is generally regarded as
the absolute state of the art.

00:06:45.135 --> 00:06:50.135
If a typical supermarket
is 50,000 square feet,

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:54.060
an Ocado unit is 350,000 square feet

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.730
and it reached tremendous efficiencies.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.589
- [Narrator] In a statement
to the Wall Street Journal,

00:06:58.589 --> 00:07:01.760
Ocado said, "Kroger has
licensed Ocado's technology

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.597
"and will be going live

00:07:03.597 --> 00:07:05.897
"with its first highly
automated warehouses

00:07:05.897 --> 00:07:07.718
"in the not too distant future."

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:11.270
Back in Queens, Rodriguez
remains confident

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.754
that once the pandemic passes,

00:07:12.754 --> 00:07:15.507
his customers will still
want the neighborhood

00:07:15.507 --> 00:07:18.240
in store experience that Billy's offers.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.830
- I'm really tryna get people here.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.120
I feel like if everything goes online,

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.120
what kind of experience
are you really gonna have?

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.090
You've gotta get out
of the house sometimes,

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.510
so its almost to make people feel at home.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.150
That's really what we wanted to do,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.323
make people feel at home at the store.

